Vue basics

Options API is preferred because Options API is much easier to read in the beginning

Next year we're going from Options API to Composition API (Composition is newer but also more complicated)

Typescript is preferred for Vue but not required

Vue prevents a lot of copied code through the use of components

UI usually only needs to be written once then called in other components

<template> <style> <script> is HTML, CSS, and Javascript respectfully, leave these be

You can use the <template> for specific components, but <style> is normally placed in a central main.css file

New Vue Project:
-Open the right directory in the terminal (right click, open in terminal)

- write npm create vue@latest

-Answer the questions

-Router and Pinia are going to be features that are included in the project

-Go to the directory for your Vue Project (cd VueProjectName)

-Run npm install OR npm i

-run npm run dev

Don't mess with the .gitignore, node_modules absolutely cannot go to Github or away from where it is.
If you mess with it it can deleted accidentally.

If node_modules or package.json give any problems of if you have problems in your project, delete those two files
and run npm install again.

npm will automatically choose the next available port (localhost), for example if you have 5173 open already
it will automatically open 5174

The most important files in a vue projects are index.html and main.js.

in the index.html you replace the contents of the div with the ID "app" as many times as needed, so you never leave
index.html if you open another "page"

in main.js you make and import your App.vue, so it can replace the div when it needs to, without this file you
dont have a Vue project. The main.js also makes sure you have routers available to change pages and a pinia
store so that you can save and send information throughout the routings (for example, login information).

You can right click on the views folder and add a new view to your Vue project, but always keep Options API clicked

Do not delete any code that it starts with

You can see if a vue project is options or composition api through checking the <script>. If it has setup
next to it, it's composition.


<script>
export default {
  name: "HomeView",
  components: {
    ProductCard
  }
}
import ProductCard from "@/components/ProductCard.vue";
</script>

this is a standard setup of a Options API component, or View. when you add a component to a view, you need to import
it, or else it won't know where the file comes from and won't load it

To call data from the initial component in your html or <template> you can use {{(data-name)}}

With props after your data you can specify what data types your data has, for example in the product card from
the tutorial you can specify that your category is an array or your name is a string, but every type is typed with
a capital letter. Every product will have these "props", or properties. So if you make a new product it will have
these automatically

if you have the props you van specify what the props are when you call the component in the
HomeView, for example the name, price, image etc. This way you can call it multiple time for multiple
products, you could also call it in a v-for list in order to show every product from either a json server or from
an sql server

v-for="member in teamMembers" :key="member.id" :member="member"

(MyComponent.vue shows how to use props in order to show information. It also uses the computed function
that does something as soon as the component is loaded)

You can also only show something if a requirement is met, for example if you have an image, show the image if not
show a default image,  like give you a specific background color if it has a class, MyComponent also has an
example of that

You can also call methods in your script, so that they can be called in your template. this is a v-on directive
but for things like buttons and such you can also use @click (v-on is longhand, @ is shorthand)

v-bind is one way data binding, v-model is two way data binding(v-model has an example in myComponent)

v-if and v-else shows something if a requirement is met, and shows something else if the requirement is not met
          <img v-if="showImage" :src="product.photo" class="card-img-top" :alt="product.description">
          <img v-else src="/src/assets/stock-profile-photo.jpg" class="card-img-top" alt="{{product.description}}">
(true is truthy and false is falsy in vue)


